{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Eye for eye.</b> The tradition of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, on this is: monetary compensation for the eye, and not the eye literally. And the proof is that above He mentioned only ״שבתו יתן״ — 'he shall give for his idleness' — and ״רפא ירפא״ — 'he shall surely heal'; and if this were done to the man who strikes his fellow just as he did to him, what would he pay thereafter, when he too requires idleness and healing? And furthermore, this one who put out his fellow's eye — if we did likewise to him and put out his eye, not all natures are equal, and perhaps the second would die from the removal of his eye because his nature is more delicate than the first's, and the Torah said ״עין תחת עין״ — 'eye for eye' — and not 'eye and life for eye'; if so, there is no equal justice and equitable law for all the natures of human beings except by means of monetary compensation. And furthermore, the verse that says (Leviticus 24:19–20) ״ואיש כי יתן מום בעמיתו כאשר עשה כן יעשה לו: שבר תחת שבר עין תחת עין שן תחת שן כאשר יתן מום באדם כן ינתן בו״ — 'And if a man puts a blemish in his fellow, as he did so shall be done to him: fracture for fracture, eye for eye, tooth for tooth; as he puts a blemish in a person, so shall be put in him' — it is impossible for this verse to be fulfilled except according to the tradition of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, as monetary compensation, and not actual removal of a limb, because it is impossible for a person to make a wound exactly like the one his fellow made in him in length and breadth and depth, and he cannot make it precisely; and if he makes it less or more, he has not fulfilled ״כאשר עשה כן יעשה לו״; rather, from this it follows that it is not literal but rather monetary compensation — the monetary value of what he did, no less and no more." "<b>And one should ask:</b> how do we fulfill the verse ״כאשר עשה כן יעשה לו״ — 'as he did, so shall be done to him' — if not by making an actual wound? For a wound is what he made; and if this one who made a wound is obligated to pay monetary compensation, how can ״כאשר עשה כן יעשה לו״ be fulfilled?" "<b>And one may say</b> that the meaning of ״כאשר עשה כן יעשה לו״ is: as he did evil, so shall evil be done to him. And the proof is from the words of Samson, who said (Judges 15:11) ״כאשר עשו לי כן עשיתי להם״ — 'as they did to me, so I did to them' — and was it not so that they took his wife, while he burned their crops? Their deed was not like his deed! Rather, certainly since they did evil one to the other it is as though the retribution was repaid in kind. And so we find in the words of the prophet who prophesies concerning Esau (Obadiah): ״כאשר עשית יעשה לך גמולך ישוב בראשך״ — 'as you have done, so shall be done to you; your recompense shall return upon your head' — ״כי כאשר שתיתם על הר קדשי״ — 'for as you drank on My holy mountain'; from this you learn from reason and from Scripture and from the words of the Sages that the meaning of ״עין תחת עין״ is not literal but rather monetary compensation. Thus wrote Rabbenu Hananel, of blessed memory." "<b>Now behold,</b> Scripture mentioned seven things: eye, tooth, hand, foot, burn, wound, bruise — each one having something that the others do not, so that it is impossible to derive one from the rest. The eye was created with him, but the tooth was not created with him and is not among the count of the 248 (רמ״ח) limbs that a person has, for every single limb of the 248 limbs has flesh and tendons and bones; therefore we could not derive tooth from eye, since the tooth is not included among the limbs but is rather one of the instruments of grinding; and so with all of them."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:51.457865860Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}